<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    TableTop Forge — Android Network Security Config.

    Replaces the old `usesCleartextTraffic="true"` flag that the
    main manifest used to have. That flag allowed cleartext to
    every host on every network — wider than we actually need,
    and the kind of thing Play Store review flags as a privacy /
    security warning.

    Intent under this config:

      * HTTPS is required by default for any host the app talks to.
        Public-internet TableTop Forge servers MUST run over HTTPS;
        users who want a remote-accessible deploy stand up a
        Cloudflare Tunnel, Tailscale Funnel, ngrok, or a self-
        hosted reverse proxy with a real cert.

      * Cleartext (HTTP) is permitted only for the LAN-private
        destinations a self-hosted GM is likely to point the app
        at on first run, where setting up TLS would mean every
        new user has to install certs on every device:

          - localhost / 127.0.0.1   — same-machine debugging
          - 10.0.2.2                — Android emulator → host
          - *.local                 — mDNS / Bonjour hostnames

        Anything else (specific RFC1918 IPv4 literals like
        192.168.x.y, public DNS names with HTTP) is rejected at
        the platform level. The Connection panel in the GM view
        explains how to set up an HTTPS endpoint if a player's
        device blocks the connection.

    Tightening fallback: if Play Store review pushes back even
    on this set, the next-tightest position is to drop the .local
    wildcard and keep just the literal loopback addresses. Keep
    the cleartext domain-config block — Play uses it as a
    structured signal that the developer has audited the
    cleartext usage, vs. the more alarming `usesCleartextTraffic="true"`
    manifest attribute which suggests "every host, anywhere".
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">local</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
